#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""heimdall-fallback -- quota-exhaustion fallback POLICY gate (state, config,
and the safety gate only -- no transport).

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
When Claude capacity is exhausted mid-task, hmd may -- ONLY if the operator
opts in -- route work to a locally-hosted OmniRoute gateway using the
OPERATOR'S OWN third-party API keys. See
docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md for the full risk analysis
(verdict: do NOT integrate directly; every constraint below is sourced from
that doc, none invented here). The Tier-1 boundary specifically is enforced
per docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md's positive-
verification design (see run_preflight): there is no config flag anywhere in
OmniRoute that disables Tier-1, so this tool verifies the real structural
control -- credential ABSENCE -- instead of trusting a self-attested boolean.

THIS TOOL IS POLICY ONLY. It computes state, config, and the safety-gate
verdict. It does NOT implement transport -- it never calls OmniRoute, never
rewrites a request, never speaks to anything but its own local reachability
probe and OmniRoute's own SQLite DB (read-only). The seam a future transport
layer must consult is `check`'s exit code. Nothing else in this repo may
treat "the operator flipped a config flag" as license to skip this gate.

THE SAFETY BOUNDARY (mirrors bin/heimdall-quota-advisor's own header -- that
tool prints options for a human; this one gates an unattended fallback, same
boundary from a different angle):
  1. NEVER enable, configure, or route through OmniRoute's "Tier 1
     Subscription (Claude Code, Codex, Copilot)" mode -- it repurposes a
     Claude Code / Codex / Copilot subscription's own auth as a backend for
     arbitrary routed traffic (assessment doc, "Tier 1 Subscription"
     section). There is no config flag that disables Tier-1 -- none exists in
     OmniRoute to positively verify (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-
     credential-isolation.md, S6) -- so this tool instead positively verifies
     the actual structural control, credential ABSENCE, across four
     independent checks, ALL required, none short-circuiting another:
       a. tier1_credential_absent -- no `provider_connections` row for
          provider 'claude' or 'claude-web' in OmniRoute's own SQLite DB (the
          real gate; S2-S3). An absent or unreadable DB is NOT a pass -- it
          is a FAIL, because absence-of-evidence is not evidence-of-absence
          for an externally-owned database this tool does not control.
       b. anthropic_model_pinned -- ANTHROPIC_MODEL must be set AND carry an
          explicit `provider/` prefix. Claude Code emits bare `claude-*` ids
          when this is unset, and OmniRoute has an explicit routing branch
          that matches a bare `claude-*` id to provider `claude` (S5). The
          pin is the second line of defense behind (a), never a substitute
          for it.
       c. prefer_claude_code_flag_off --
          OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS must stay
          unset/false. Default is false; this is a Tier-1 WIDENING knob, the
          opposite polarity from what an operator would expect of a variable
          with "prefer" in its name (S3, "must not do" #6).
       d. no_delegated_sidecar -- no delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI, or
          Dario -- OmniRoute's own source names it a "Claude-subscription
          proxy") may be configured. A `mode` of 'cliproxyapi', 'dario', or
          'fallback' on an upstream_proxy_config row can reach a Claude
          subscription while OmniRoute's own provider_connections table
          holds no Tier-1 token at all, which would make check (a) alone
          insufficient (S1a path 8, residual risk 2). Confirmed against a
          live OmniRoute 3.8.51 (d82b682) install that this lives on
          upstream_proxy_config, NOT provider_connections (which has no
          `mode` column at all) -- see SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES. An
          upstream_proxy_config table that does not exist AT ALL is a PASS,
          never a FAIL, when provider_connections (check (a), just above) is
          itself readable -- a schema predating migration
          138_dario_fallback_backend.sql structurally cannot have a
          delegated sidecar, which is a stronger, positive fact than
          "unknown". Genuine unreadability (provider_connections ALSO
          failing) still fails closed exactly as every other branch here
          does -- see run_preflight's own comment for the full reasoning,
          and test/issue-loop-claude-fix-fallback.test.sh's case (c) for the
          real caller whose own hermetic fixture is exactly this DB shape.
     Fail closed on all four: refusing is always safer than proceeding on an
     unknown.
  2. NEVER enable OmniRoute's opt-in root-CA MITM ("TPROXY") decrypt mode. A
     config that mentions it in any form (see FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS) is
     treated as corrupt and forced back to state=off.
  3. NEVER route on OmniRoute's own ToS-conflict flag alone. OmniRoute's docs
     call that flag advisory, not a routing gate, and still route through
     flagged providers by default. hmd applies its OWN gate instead:
     target_provider_allowed refuses any provider named in config's
     `tos_flagged_providers`, added to a BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS
     starting set that is EMPTY by design (owner decision, 2026-08-26 -- see
     that set's own comment for the full reasoning: the prior ~30-entry set
     was sourced from the assessment doc's Section 2, a table built for
     OmniRoute's free-tier POOLED-credential routing, not this tool's
     operator's-own-key model, and copying it verbatim left the fallback
     feature permanently inert). Config may still only ADD to this set,
     never remove or override an entry that IS present -- an operator who
     wants a provider denied still names it in `tos_flagged_providers`.
  4. Operator's own API keys ONLY. This tool never reads, reuses, or forwards
     the Claude Code OAuth session -- it only checks whether an
     operator-named env var is SET (see run_preflight's "operator_key"
     check). It never reads the value, and an operator_key_env that names a
     Claude/Anthropic credential is itself a preflight failure (see
     FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS).
  5. NEVER print or log a secret. Every subcommand reports key PRESENCE
     ("configured" / "absent") only, never a value. Follows the non-TTY
     secret-refusal precedent in bin/heimdall-invite (secrets withheld by
     default, never inferred from an ambient variable of the same name) --
     this tool goes further still: it has no print-the-secret override at
     all, because it never has a reason to print one.
  6. `off` is the default for a fresh install AND for any absent or corrupt
     config. A corrupt config must never be silently read as `on` -- see
     load_config()'s fail-closed parse.
  7. NEVER treat OmniRoute's `blockedProviders` setting as evidence of
     anything. Its own UI copy is "Hide specific providers from the
     /v1/models response" -- it is consulted only in model-listing/search
     paths and is never read by getProviderCredentials() (S3 point 1, S6). A
     same-named key placed in THIS tool's own config is already dropped
     silently by load_config()'s allowlist copy; cmd_check additionally
     warns on stderr if it finds one, so that silence is never mistaken for
     the field having done something.
  8. FOUR STATES, one safety boundary. `off` (default, never route), `on` (a
     CAPABILITY-TIER routing decision -- route only LOW-LEVEL work, never an
     exhaustion reaction), `auto` (the ORIGINAL exhaustion/pre-exhaustion
     reaction -- route only when preflight passes, else WAIT rather than
     block), and `switch` (route EVERYTHING, tier-blind, still gated by the
     same preflight as every other state). None of the four ever changes
     WHETHER routing is safe -- points 1-7 above apply identically in all
     four; `switch` changes WHAT routes, never whether a failing
     Tier-1/model-pin/sidecar/ToS check is survivable. "Low-level" reuses
     this repo's OWN existing task-tier vocabulary rather than inventing a
     second one -- see agents/heimdall.md's Model Routing table (haiku:
     "lint, format, rename, simple config") and CLAUDE.md's "Model routing
     (2026-08-11 directive)" (opus reserved for adjudication:
     reviewer/verifier/security-auditor; sonnet is the default coding tier).
     `on` maps to the haiku tier ONLY -- sonnet (default coding) and the
     adjudication roles (always opus; capability is load-bearing there) NEVER
     route under `on`, regardless of how the caller spells the tier. `check
     --tier T` (T in haiku|sonnet|opus|reviewer|verifier|security-auditor,
     the last three being direct adjudication-role aliases for opus) asks
     this per-task question; bare `check` (no --tier) asks the ORIGINAL,
     tier-blind question and is completely unaffected by this whole
     paragraph -- see bin/lib/issue_loop.py, the one existing caller, which
     is preserved byte-for-byte (it never passes --tier).
  9. `switch` MUST be unmistakable. It is the only state that risks
     quality-sensitive work landing on a provider with no no-train guarantee
     (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-headroom-inpath-measurement.md's 337x
     prompt-cache-vs-compression gap is why routing HEALTHY traffic through
     OmniRoute is a real cost, not a free option). `status` and `check` both
     print an explicit, impossible-to-miss warning whenever state is
     'switch' -- never folded quietly into the normal state line.
  10. A no-auth/free target_provider needs NO operator_key_env, and that is
      NOT a gap -- it mirrors OmniRoute's own architecture. Credential
      resolution reads provider_connections for every provider EXCEPT a
      fixed set served via a synthetic "noauth" connection instead
      (NOAUTH_PROVIDERS, sourced verbatim from docs/analysis/2026-08-25-
      omniroute-credential-isolation.md's own citation of OmniRoute source;
      `claude`/`claude-web` are explicitly NOT in it, so this can never
      collide with point 1's Tier-1 boundary). The operator_key check passes
      keylessly ONLY for a target_provider in that list, or one the operator
      has explicitly named in config's `noauth_providers` -- an
      addition-only declaration for a provider this tool's hardcoded list
      has not caught up with yet, never a guess this tool makes on its own.
      Two things stay true regardless: (a) even a no-auth provider still
      refuses an operator_key_env naming a Claude/Anthropic credential
      (point 4 is absolute, not conditioned on necessity), and (b)
      target_provider_allowed's OWN ToS deny-list check is completely
      independent -- a provider can be no-auth AND still operator-denied
      (e.g. an operator who lists `duckduckgo-web` in `tos_flagged_providers`
      still gets it refused even though it is also in NOAUTH_PROVIDERS --
      see test/heimdall-fallback.test.sh). Keyless routes are OmniRoute's
      free/no-auth tier, the SAME weakest-contractual-terms category the
      ToS-conflict flag in point 3 exists for
      (docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md) -- `status` and
      `check` both say this once, plainly, whenever it applies, so the
      operator sees it without having to remember it.

  11. state=="auto" is the one state that is actually exhaustion-gated.
      It best-effort-consults bin/heimdall-session-usage's own
      ~95%-of-session-limit pre-exhaustion verdict and treats it as a
      SECOND, NECESSARY condition for ROUTE: `check` prints VERDICT: ROUTE
      under auto only when the preflight passes AND heimdall-session-usage
      reports "crossed". A passing preflight with capacity still available
      (verdict "under") WAITs instead of routing -- there is no reason to
      route off Anthropic while quota remains, and doing so anyway is pure
      cost/latency loss (cache is 91-95% of the value and OmniRoute drops
      cache_control; see
      docs/analysis/2026-08-25-headroom-inpath-measurement.md). This is a
      NECESSARY condition ADDED to the existing gate, never a substitute for
      it: crossing 95% still authorizes nothing on its own -- every
      preflight check above (Tier-1, model pin, sidecar, provider
      allow-list, all of it) still has to pass first, exactly as before; a
      failing preflight WAITs regardless of session_usage, crossed or not.

      heimdall-session-usage's own verdict is one of four: "unconfigured" /
      "unknown" / "under" / "crossed" -- all four are load-bearing and kept
      textually distinct on purpose. ROUTE requires an explicit `verdict ==
      "crossed"` allow-list match, never a `verdict != "under"` deny-list --
      so "unconfigured" and "unknown" can never be silently promoted into
      looking like a confirmed threshold-crossing. "unknown" (tool missing,
      timed out, or returned something this cannot parse) is deliberately
      treated the SAME as "under" for the routing decision -- both WAIT --
      but for a DIFFERENT, explicitly reasoned cause (see the dedicated
      `session_verdict == "unknown"` branch in _verdict()): when capacity
      cannot be measured at all, routing off Anthropic on the strength of a
      signal that was never confirmed would be exactly the fail-OPEN
      behavior this file exists to avoid (see "MUST FAIL CLOSED" below, and
      tier1_credential_absent / no_delegated_sidecar's identical "absence is
      not proof" reasoning). Waiting on "unknown" risks a delayed fallback
      if the measurement stays broken; routing on "unknown" risks burning
      fallback-provider capacity, and a cold/degraded cache, while Anthropic
      quota may still have been available -- this file already fails closed
      everywhere else, so it fails closed here too. "unknown" is NEVER read
      as "under" -- it stays its own reasoned branch with its own [INFO]
      wording; the two simply arrive at the same WAIT outcome by separate,
      explicit paths, not by being conflated into one. Best effort, no
      exceptions: a missing, non-executable, timed-out or malformed
      heimdall-session-usage collapses to "unknown" and changes nothing else
      about the OTHER checks -- see _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict().

MUST FAIL CLOSED -- the deliberate inverse of heimdall-quota-advisor's "MUST
FAIL OPEN". The advisor's job is to never cry wolf: an uncertain exhaustion
signal must stay silent (exit quietly, print nothing). This tool's job is
the opposite: an uncertain SAFETY signal must never say "safe to route". Any
internal error, missing field, or unparsable config resolves to the LEAST
permissive verdict available for the current state (REFUSE under off/on,
WAIT under auto) -- never to ROUTE. Two inverse philosophies for two
different problems, not a contradiction: one gates a print statement, the
other gates unattended, keyed network egress.

NO AUTO-SWITCH CONTRADICTION. heimdall-quota-advisor documents that it
"never auto-switches ANY provider for the operator. Ever." This tool's
`on`/`auto` states do not contradict that: (a) `off` is the default
everywhere, so a fresh install has zero auto-switch behavior anywhere in
this repo; (b) `on`/`auto` are reachable ONLY via an explicit
`heimdall-fallback set on|auto` the operator runs themselves -- hmd never
flips this on its own; (c) this tool does not even perform a switch -- it
only computes whether a future transport layer would be ALLOWED to.

EXHAUSTION vs. OVERLOAD -- NOT THE SAME THING, NOT HANDLED HERE. A transient
529 / overloaded_error / 429 is a RETRYABLE condition with its own mechanism
(exponential backoff in bin/lib/hmd-claude-retry.sh, feeding the AIMD spawn
throttle in bin/lib/pressure_control.py). Genuine quota/session exhaustion is
a different condition entirely (classified by bin/lib/quota_stop.py,
surfaced via bin/heimdall-quota-advisor). This tool concerns ONLY the
latter. It has no opinion on retries and must never be consulted for one.

USAGE
-----
  heimdall-fallback status [--json]            current state + would-preflight-pass, secret-free
  heimdall-fallback set {off,on,auto,switch}   persist a new state (off is always safe)
  heimdall-fallback check [--tier T]           run the preflight; exit code IS the verdict.
                                                --tier asks a CAPABILITY-TIER question (only
                                                meaningful under state=on; T is one of haiku,
                                                sonnet, opus, or an adjudication-role alias --
                                                reviewer, verifier, security-auditor -- for
                                                opus). Omit --tier for the ORIGINAL, tier-blind
                                                question every existing caller
                                                (bin/lib/issue_loop.py) already asks -- see
                                                header point 8.
  heimdall-fallback where                      print the config file path

Global flags (before the subcommand -- matches bin/heimdall-pressure /
bin/heimdall-metric):
  --repo PATH   repo root containing .heimdall/ (default: .)
  --strict      tests only; callers never pass this

CONFIG  <repo>/.heimdall/fallback.json -- gitignored by the blanket
`.heimdall/*` rule in .gitignore; no committed .example needed since every
field below defaults to the safe value:
  {
    "schema": 1,
    "state": "off",                        # off | on | auto | switch -- anything else
                                            # reads as off (header point 6)
    "operator_key_env": "",                # NAME of an env var holding the operator's
                                            # OWN OmniRoute/provider key -- never a value.
                                            # NOT required when target_provider is a
                                            # no-auth/free provider -- see header point 10
    "endpoint": "http://127.0.0.1:20128",  # must resolve local; OmniRoute's documented
                                            # default self-hosted gateway port
    "omniroute_db_path": "",               # path to OmniRoute's own SQLite DB; empty ->
                                            # $DATA_DIR/storage.sqlite or ~/.omniroute/
                                            # storage.sqlite (positive Tier-1 credential
                                            # check -- see header point 1a)
    "cliproxyapi_dir": "",                 # path to CLIProxyAPI's config dir; empty ->
                                            # $CLIPROXYAPI_CONFIG_DIR or ~/.cli-proxy-api
                                            # (delegated-sidecar check -- header point 1d)
    "target_provider": "",                 # provider name; checked against the deny list
    "tos_flagged_providers": [],           # operator ADDITIONS to the built-in deny
                                            # list only -- can never remove a built-in
    "noauth_providers": []                 # operator ADDITIONS to the built-in no-auth
                                            # (keyless) provider list only -- see header
                                            # point 10; never removes a built-in entry
  }

EXIT CODES
  status, where : always 0 (never-fail-caller telemetry reads)
  set           : 0 (even on bad input) unless --strict -> 2 usage / 3 write-fail
  check         : 0 = ROUTE  (every preflight check passed; under state=on with
                              --tier given, the tier must also resolve to haiku)
                  1 = REFUSE (state is 'off'; OR state is 'on' and a preflight
                              check failed, or --tier was given and does not
                              resolve to haiku; OR state is 'switch' and a
                              preflight check failed)
                  2 = WAIT   (state is 'auto' and a preflight check failed --
                              print the reasons, do not route blind; tier is
                              NEVER consulted here -- auto is an exhaustion
                              reaction, not a capability decision)
  argument-parsing errors (any subcommand, including an unrecognized --tier
  value) : 0 unless --strict -> 2
  unexpected internal error (outer safety net; check() fails closed on its
  own and should never actually reach this) : 0 unless --strict -> 3
"""

import argparse
import ipaddress
import json
import os
import socket
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
import urllib.parse

SCHEMA = 1
VALID_STATES = ("off", "on", "auto", "switch")

DEFAULT_CFG = {
    "schema": SCHEMA,
    "state": "off",
    "operator_key_env": "",
    "endpoint": "http://127.0.0.1:20128",
    "omniroute_db_path": "",
    "cliproxyapi_dir": "",
    "target_provider": "",
    "tos_flagged_providers": [],
    "noauth_providers": [],
}

# EMPTY BY DESIGN (owner decision, 2026-08-26). hmd's OWN gate is unchanged --
# target_provider_allowed still refuses any provider named in
# `tos_flagged_providers` (deny = this set | config's additions, see
# run_preflight below) -- only the STARTING set that field adds to has
# changed, from ~30 entries to none. Config may still only ADD to this set,
# never remove or override an entry that IS present: an operator who wants a
# specific provider denied still lists it in `tos_flagged_providers` and it
# is refused exactly as before -- see header point 3.
#
# WHY: the previous ~30-entry set was copied verbatim from
# docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md, Section 2 -- a table
# OmniRoute's own maintainers built to flag providers whose ToS prohibits
# being PROXIED/RESOLD through OmniRoute's free-tier, POOLED-credential
# routing (e.g. groq: "prohibits reselling, sublicensing, or distributing
# API access"; together: "prohibits transferring, distributing, reselling...
# on a s[tandalone basis]"). That is a real conflict for OmniRoute's free
# tier, but this tool never touches OmniRoute's shared credentials at all --
# header point 4 is OPERATOR'S OWN API keys ONLY, a direct pinned request
# through a locally-hosted gateway the operator runs themselves. Copying the
# free-tier table into THIS tool's deny list conflated "OmniRoute's free-tier
# PROXIED access to provider X" with "provider X itself": using the
# operator's own key against, say, Mistral's official first-party API is not
# a ToS conflict with anyone. docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-
# isolation.md S5 independently found this exact shape to be the
# structurally CLEAN one -- its own worked example is a pinned
# `mistral/mistral-large-latest` request, which "never enters the
# multi-provider candidate pool" and (chatCore.ts:2733) has "no other target
# to fail over to," i.e. it cannot cascade into Tier-1 either. With the old
# table covering nearly every real provider name by design (the assessment
# doc flagged ~30 across its "caution" and lower-confidence tiers),
# target_provider_allowed could never pass and the fallback feature was
# permanently inert regardless of an operator's own choice. The operator
# turns fallback on EXPLICITLY (`heimdall-fallback set on|auto|switch` is a
# deliberate act hmd never performs itself -- see "NO AUTO-SWITCH
# CONTRADICTION" above) and has already accepted the data-handling tradeoff
# (2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md's disqualifier-1 finding; the
# credential-isolation doc's own "OUT OF SCOPE" explicitly declines to
# re-litigate "data-handling/no-train/retention posture of Mistral or LLM7 --
# owner has explicitly accepted that risk"). A built-in list overriding that
# explicit, informed choice is hmd deciding for the operator.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT TOUCH: the Tier-1 boundary -- tier1_credential_absent,
# anthropic_model_pinned, prefer_claude_code_flag_off, and
# no_delegated_sidecar (header point 1a-1d) -- is what actually protects the
# operator's own Anthropic account, and none of those four checks reads this
# set at all. See test/heimdall-fallback.test.sh's dedicated falsifiers
# proving a `claude` provider_connections row is still refused, and
# state=switch still REFUSEs on a failing Tier-1 check, with this set empty.
BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS = frozenset()

# Providers OmniRoute serves via a SYNTHETIC "noauth" connection instead of a
# provider_connections credential row -- sourced verbatim (READ, not guessed)
# from docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md's own
# citation of OmniRoute's NOAUTH_PROVIDERS / no-auth WEB_COOKIE_PROVIDERS
# list. `claude`/`claude-web` are explicitly NOT in it (that doc's own
# emphasis), so this can never collide with the Tier-1 boundary above. An
# operator key is genuinely not required to route to one of these -- that is
# a property of OmniRoute's own architecture, not a gap in this tool. See
# header point 10.
NOAUTH_PROVIDERS = frozenset({
    "devin-cli-agentic", "opencode", "duckduckgo-web", "cloudflare-playground",
    "felo-web", "theoldllm", "chipotle", "veoaifree-web", "auggie", "zcode",
    "codex-app-server", "uncloseai", "aihorde",
})

# One-time, plain explanation surfaced in BOTH `status` and `check` whenever
# the pinned target_provider resolves to a no-auth route (never nagged, never
# repeated beyond the one line) -- the owner's own framing: keyless does not
# mean risk-free, it means OmniRoute's free/no-auth tier, the same weakest-
# contractual-terms category the ToS-conflict flag exists for (see
# docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md and header point 10).
NOAUTH_ROUTE_NOTE_FMT = (
    "target_provider '%s' is served keyless via OmniRoute's own no-auth "
    "connection path -- no operator key is required. Keyless routes are "
    "OmniRoute's free/no-auth tier, the same weakest-contractual-terms "
    "category the ToS-conflict deny list exists for; target_provider_allowed "
    "still applies independently and is not weakened by this."
)

# Best-effort session-usage pre-exhaustion consultation for state=="auto" --
# see header point 11. Four verdicts, all load-bearing: "unknown" must never
# be read as "under", so the note text below is deliberately distinct for
# every one of the four, and _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict() only ever
# returns a value from this exact tuple -- any other outcome it sees
# collapses to "unknown" there, never invents a fifth state here. "crossed"
# is the ONE value _verdict() allow-lists as a necessary (not sufficient)
# condition for ROUTE under auto -- see _verdict() itself.
SESSION_USAGE_VERDICTS = ("unconfigured", "unknown", "under", "crossed")
SESSION_USAGE_NOTE_FMT = {
    "crossed": (
        "pre-exhaustion threshold CROSSED (~95% of session limit) -- the "
        "checks above are still the ONLY thing that decides ROUTE/WAIT/"
        "REFUSE; this authorizes nothing on its own"
    ),
    "under": "under the pre-exhaustion threshold",
    "unconfigured": "tracking not configured (see heimdall-session-usage)",
    "unknown": "could not be determined -- NOT assumed to be under threshold",
}

# Any of these keys appearing in an on-disk config (any value) is how
# OmniRoute's MITM mode or a blind trust of its advisory ToS flag would be
# spelled. Their mere presence marks the whole config corrupt. See header
# points 2 and 3.
FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS = frozenset({
    "mitm", "mitm_enabled", "tproxy", "tproxy_enabled",
    "root_ca", "decrypt_mode", "trust_omniroute_tos_flag",
})

# An operator_key_env naming a Claude/Anthropic/Claude-Code credential is
# never "the operator's own third-party key" -- see header point 4.
FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS = ("ANTHROPIC", "CLAUDE")

LOCAL_HOSTNAMES = frozenset({"localhost", "127.0.0.1", "::1"})

# Tier-1 subscription providers in OmniRoute's own provider_connections table
# -- docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3's sweep
# list narrowed to the two that are the SAME Anthropic account (Claude Code
# OAuth and a claude.ai session cookie respectively). See header point 1a.
TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS = ("claude", "claude-web")

# upstream_proxy_config.mode values that mean the retry/primary leg for a
# provider is handled by a delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI or Dario) rather
# than OmniRoute's own native call -- OmniRoute itself can hold zero Tier-1
# tokens while this is still true. See header point 1d, audit S1a path 8 and
# residual risk 2. Sourced verbatim from a live OmniRoute 3.8.51 (d82b682)
# install's own src/lib/db/migrations/138_dario_fallback_backend.sql: "mode
# is a free TEXT column already ('native' | 'cliproxyapi' | 'dario' |
# 'fallback')" -- 'native' is the only non-delegating value; 'fallback' means
# the RETRY leg goes to whichever of cliproxyapi/dario `fallback_backend`
# names (default 'cliproxyapi'), so it is delegating regardless of which
# backend it names. This check ignores the row's own `enabled` flag on
# purpose -- fail-closed treats a configured-but-disabled delegation the same
# as an active one, since re-enabling it is a single flag flip this tool does
# not control or continuously observe. NOTE this is the table/column the
# check ACTUALLY queries -- provider_connections (queried above for Tier-1)
# has no 'mode' column on a real install at all (confirmed: 46 real columns,
# zero named 'mode'); an earlier version of this check queried it there by
# mistake, inferred from the audit's prose rather than the real schema, and
# always passed vacuously.
SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES = frozenset({"cliproxyapi", "dario", "fallback"})

# Values that count as "the widening flag is off" for
# OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS -- unset and
# every common falsy spelling. Anything else (including "1", "true", "yes")
# fails the check. See header point 1c.
_ENV_FLAG_OFF_VALUES = frozenset({"", "0", "false", "no", "off"})

# "Low-level" reuses THIS repo's existing task-tier vocabulary rather than
# inventing a second one -- agents/heimdall.md's Model Routing table (haiku:
# "lint, format, rename, simple config, doc-sync") and CLAUDE.md's "Model
# routing (2026-08-11 directive)" (opus reserved for adjudication: reviewer,
# verifier, security-auditor; sonnet is the default coding tier). See header
# point 8. The adjudication roles are direct opus aliases so "adjudication
# never routes under on" is an explicit, testable guarantee, not an accident
# of opus's tier string alone. NOTE: .planning/settings.json's
# `model_routing` field (an alternate source the original brief also named)
# does not exist anywhere in this repo -- confirmed by direct search; this
# vocabulary comes from agents/heimdall.md and CLAUDE.md instead, the only
# place it is actually defined.
TIER_ALIASES = {
    "haiku": "haiku",
    "sonnet": "sonnet",
    "opus": "opus",
    "reviewer": "opus",
    "verifier": "opus",
    "security-auditor": "opus",
}
LOW_LEVEL_TIER = "haiku"


class Rejected(Exception):
    """Raised by _Parser.error() instead of argparse's default sys.exit(2)."""


class _Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    def error(self, message):
        raise Rejected(message)


def _cfg_path(repo):
    return os.path.join(repo, ".heimdall", "fallback.json")


def load_config(repo):
    """Fail-closed config load. Absent, unreadable, non-dict, or containing a
    FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS entry all resolve to a fresh DEFAULT_CFG copy
    (state off) -- never a partially-trusted merge. See header point 6.
    Returns (cfg, corrupt_reason); corrupt_reason is None when the on-disk
    config (or its absence) was clean.
    """
    path = _cfg_path(repo)
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            raw = json.load(fh)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), "config file is unreadable or not valid JSON"
    if not isinstance(raw, dict):
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), "config is not a JSON object"
    forbidden_present = sorted(k for k in raw if k in FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS)
    if forbidden_present:
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), "config contains forbidden key(s): " + ", ".join(forbidden_present)

    cfg = dict(DEFAULT_CFG)
    for key in DEFAULT_CFG:
        if key in raw:
            cfg[key] = raw[key]
    if cfg.get("state") not in VALID_STATES:
        cfg["state"] = "off"
    if isinstance(cfg.get("tos_flagged_providers"), list):
        cfg["tos_flagged_providers"] = [p for p in cfg["tos_flagged_providers"] if isinstance(p, str)]
    else:
        cfg["tos_flagged_providers"] = []
    if isinstance(cfg.get("noauth_providers"), list):
        cfg["noauth_providers"] = [p for p in cfg["noauth_providers"] if isinstance(p, str)]
    else:
        cfg["noauth_providers"] = []
    for key in ("operator_key_env", "endpoint", "target_provider", "omniroute_db_path", "cliproxyapi_dir"):
        if not isinstance(cfg.get(key), str):
            cfg[key] = DEFAULT_CFG[key]
    return cfg, None


def save_config(repo, cfg):
    path = _cfg_path(repo)
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        json.dump(cfg, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
        fh.write("\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def _is_local_endpoint(endpoint):
    try:
        parsed = urllib.parse.urlparse(endpoint)
    except ValueError:
        return False
    host = parsed.hostname
    if not host:
        return False
    if host.lower() in LOCAL_HOSTNAMES:
        return True
    try:
        return ipaddress.ip_address(host).is_loopback
    except ValueError:
        return False


def _endpoint_reachable(endpoint):
    """Real loopback TCP probe -- stdlib socket only, nothing else.
    Overridable ONLY for hermetic tests via
    HEIMDALL_FALLBACK_ASSUME_REACHABLE (nothing outside
    test/heimdall-fallback.test.sh sets this in this repo)."""
    override = os.environ.get("HEIMDALL_FALLBACK_ASSUME_REACHABLE")
    if override is not None:
        return override == "1"
    try:
        parsed = urllib.parse.urlparse(endpoint)
        host = parsed.hostname
        port = parsed.port or (443 if parsed.scheme == "https" else 80)
        if not host:
            return False
        with socket.create_connection((host, port), timeout=0.75):
            return True
    except OSError:
        return False


def _resolve_omniroute_db_path(cfg):
    """Where to find OmniRoute's own SQLite DB for the credential-absence
    check (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3).
    Priority: explicit cfg field -> DATA_DIR env var (OmniRoute's own
    override, src/lib/dataPaths.ts) -> the documented default ~/.omniroute.
    XDG_CONFIG_HOME is a second documented override but its exact path-join
    semantics are not confirmed in the audit, so it is deliberately not
    replicated here -- an unresolved limitation, not a silent guess."""
    configured = cfg.get("omniroute_db_path") or ""
    if configured:
        return os.path.expanduser(configured)
    data_dir = os.environ.get("DATA_DIR") or ""
    if data_dir:
        return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(data_dir), "storage.sqlite")
    return os.path.expanduser(os.path.join("~", ".omniroute", "storage.sqlite"))


def _resolve_cliproxyapi_dir(cfg):
    """Where CLIProxyAPI's own config directory would live if installed --
    the one real filesystem->Claude-credential route (audit S1a, path 8).
    Priority mirrors CLIProxyAPI's own resolution order exactly: cfg field ->
    CLIPROXYAPI_CONFIG_DIR env var -> ~/.cli-proxy-api default (quoted
    verbatim from OmniRoute's own cliProxyConfigDir())."""
    configured = cfg.get("cliproxyapi_dir") or ""
    if configured:
        return os.path.expanduser(configured)
    env_dir = os.environ.get("CLIPROXYAPI_CONFIG_DIR") or ""
    if env_dir:
        return os.path.expanduser(env_dir)
    return os.path.expanduser(os.path.join("~", ".cli-proxy-api"))


def _resolve_noauth_providers(cfg):
    """The built-in NOAUTH_PROVIDERS set plus any operator ADDITIONS from
    config's `noauth_providers` -- same additions-only shape as
    tos_flagged_providers (config can never remove a built-in entry, only
    name one more). An addition is the operator's own factual declaration
    ("I know this specific provider is keyless") for a provider this tool's
    hardcoded, sourced list has not caught up with yet -- never a guess this
    tool makes on its own. See header point 10."""
    return NOAUTH_PROVIDERS | {
        p.strip().lower() for p in cfg.get("noauth_providers", []) if isinstance(p, str)
    }


def _target_provider_is_noauth(cfg):
    """Whether cfg's pinned target_provider is a no-auth route -- see
    _resolve_noauth_providers and header point 10. An empty target_provider
    is never no-auth (nothing is pinned to check)."""
    provider = (cfg.get("target_provider") or "").strip().lower()
    return bool(provider) and provider in _resolve_noauth_providers(cfg)


def _open_omniroute_db_ro(path):
    """Open OmniRoute's SQLite DB strictly read-only via a URI connection --
    this tool must never create or mutate the operator's real database, not
    even by the accidental create-if-missing default of sqlite3.connect()."""
    uri = "file:%s?mode=ro" % urllib.parse.quote(path)
    return sqlite3.connect(uri, uri=True, timeout=1.0)


def _query_omniroute(path, sql, params=()):
    """Run one read-only query against the OmniRoute DB. Returns (rows,
    error) -- error is None on success, and rows is None whenever error is
    set. A failed connect (e.g. not a SQLite file at all) and a failed
    execute (e.g. a missing table/column) collapse to this same shape --
    callers must always fail closed on error, never treat None rows as an
    empty result."""
    try:
        conn = _open_omniroute_db_ro(path)
    except sqlite3.Error as exc:
        return None, str(exc)
    try:
        rows = conn.execute(sql, params).fetchall()
        return rows, None
    except sqlite3.Error as exc:
        return None, str(exc)
    finally:
        conn.close()


def _blocked_providers_note(repo):
    """blockedProviders is an OmniRoute DASHBOARD setting that hides a
    provider from /v1/models -- it is NEVER consulted by
    getProviderCredentials() and never stops routing (docs/analysis/2026-08-
    25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3, S6). If an operator has placed a
    same-named key in THIS tool's own config -- most plausibly believing it
    acts as a kill switch here -- load_config()'s allowlist copy already
    drops it silently (it is not a DEFAULT_CFG key); this returns a warning
    string so that silence is never mistaken for the field having done
    something. See header point 7."""
    path = _cfg_path(repo)
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            raw = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(raw, dict):
        return None
    for key in ("blockedProviders", "blocked_providers"):
        if key in raw:
            return (
                "config key '%s' is set but is never evidence of anything here -- it "
                "only hides a provider from OmniRoute's /v1/models listing and is not "
                "consulted by credential resolution; it has been ignored" % key
            )
    return None


def _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict(repo):
    """Best-effort consult of bin/heimdall-session-usage's own pre-exhaustion
    verdict (~95% of session limit) -- see header point 11. Feeds TWO things
    for state=="auto": the [INFO] line, AND _verdict()'s own ROUTE-vs-WAIT
    decision, where "crossed" is a necessary (not sufficient) condition for
    ROUTE. It does NOT feed anything for off/on/switch -- those states never
    call this function at all (see cmd_check).

    Every failure mode (missing binary, non-executable, timeout, non-JSON
    stdout, a JSON body with no recognized "verdict") is swallowed HERE,
    locally, and collapses to the tool's own pre-existing "unknown" bucket --
    never a fifth state, and never left to escape to cmd_check's outer
    fail-closed net, which would incorrectly turn an already-computed
    ROUTE/WAIT into a REFUSE. This consultation must never change any
    *individual preflight check's* own ok/detail -- each of those is computed
    independently in run_preflight() and is untouched by this function.
    _verdict()'s overall ROUTE/WAIT decision under auto is a separate,
    additional gate this function's return value DOES feed -- that is the
    entire point of it existing (header point 11), not a side effect to
    guard against. heimdall-session-usage's own `status` exits 1 for a
    genuine CROSSED verdict -- that is not an error, so success is judged by
    whether stdout parses as JSON with a recognized "verdict", never by the
    return code.
    """
    exe = os.environ.get("HEIMDALL_FALLBACK_SESSION_USAGE_BIN") or os.path.join(
        os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "heimdall-session-usage"
    )
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [exe, "--repo", repo, "status", "--json"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=3,
        )
    except Exception:
        return "unknown"
    try:
        payload = json.loads(proc.stdout)
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return "unknown"
    verdict = payload.get("verdict") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if verdict not in SESSION_USAGE_VERDICTS:
        return "unknown"
    return verdict


def run_preflight(cfg):
    """Every gated check runs and reports independently, in a fixed order --
    a failure at 3am names exactly what to fix, never just "preflight
    failed". Order mirrors the header's numbered safety boundary. Returns a
    list of (name, ok, reason) triples; reason is "" when ok is True.
    """
    checks = []

    state = cfg.get("state")
    checks.append((
        "state",
        state != "off",
        "" if state != "off" else "fallback state is 'off' (heimdall-fallback set on|auto|switch)",
    ))

    key_env = cfg.get("operator_key_env") or ""
    target_provider_raw = cfg.get("target_provider") or ""
    if key_env and any(marker in key_env.upper() for marker in FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS):
        # Absolute regardless of no-auth status -- see header point 4/10: the
        # Claude Code OAuth session may never be reused as a fallback key,
        # even for a provider that would otherwise need no key at all.
        checks.append((
            "operator_key", False,
            "operator_key_env '%s' names a Claude/Anthropic credential -- the Claude Code "
            "OAuth session may never be reused as a fallback key" % key_env,
        ))
    elif _target_provider_is_noauth(cfg):
        checks.append(("operator_key", True, NOAUTH_ROUTE_NOTE_FMT % target_provider_raw))
    elif not key_env:
        checks.append(("operator_key", False, "no operator_key_env configured -- an operator-owned key is required"))
    elif not os.environ.get(key_env):
        checks.append(("operator_key", False, "env var '%s' (operator_key_env) is not set" % key_env))
    else:
        checks.append(("operator_key", True, ""))

    endpoint = cfg.get("endpoint") or ""
    local_ok = bool(endpoint) and _is_local_endpoint(endpoint)
    checks.append((
        "endpoint_local", local_ok,
        "" if local_ok else "endpoint '%s' is not a loopback address -- fallback only ever "
                             "targets a locally-hosted gateway" % (endpoint or "(empty)"),
    ))

    if local_ok:
        reachable_ok = _endpoint_reachable(endpoint)
        reachable_reason = "" if reachable_ok else "endpoint '%s' is not reachable (is the local gateway running?)" % endpoint
    else:
        reachable_ok = False
        reachable_reason = "skipped -- endpoint is not local (see endpoint_local)"
    checks.append(("endpoint_reachable", reachable_ok, reachable_reason))

    # -- Tier-1 credential-absence + delegated-sidecar-in-DB share one DB open
    # (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3/S6; see
    # header point 1a/1d). Absent or unreadable DB is a FAIL for BOTH, never
    # a silent pass -- see header point 1.
    db_path = _resolve_omniroute_db_path(cfg)
    if not os.path.exists(db_path):
        tier1_ok = False
        tier1_reason = (
            "OmniRoute DB not found at '%s' -- absence is not proof of an empty "
            "table, so this refuses rather than passing an unverifiable check "
            "(docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3)" % db_path
        )
        sidecar_db_ok = False
        sidecar_db_reason = (
            "OmniRoute DB not found at '%s' -- cannot query upstream_proxy_config for a "
            "delegated-sidecar row, so a delegated sidecar cannot be ruled out" % db_path
        )
    else:
        provider_rows, provider_err = _query_omniroute(
            db_path,
            "SELECT DISTINCT provider FROM provider_connections WHERE provider IN (?, ?)",
            TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS,
        )
        if provider_err is not None:
            tier1_ok = False
            tier1_reason = (
                "OmniRoute DB at '%s' is unreadable or malformed (%s) -- an unreadable "
                "DB can never positively verify credential absence" % (db_path, provider_err)
            )
        elif provider_rows:
            found = ", ".join(sorted(row[0] for row in provider_rows))
            tier1_ok = False
            tier1_reason = (
                "OmniRoute DB has an active Tier-1 provider_connections row (%s) -- "
                "Tier-1 repurposes that credential as a routed backend" % found
            )
        else:
            tier1_ok = True
            tier1_reason = ""

        # upstream_proxy_config, NOT provider_connections -- confirmed against
        # a live OmniRoute 3.8.51 (d82b682) install: provider_connections has
        # 46 real columns and none named 'mode'. mode/fallback_backend live on
        # upstream_proxy_config (src/lib/db/migrations/138_dario_fallback_
        # backend.sql). See SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES for the mode vocabulary.
        #
        # The table's mere ABSENCE is deliberately NOT the same failure mode
        # as "this DB could not be read". test/issue-loop-claude-fix-
        # fallback.test.sh's case (c) -- a real, existing caller's own
        # hermetic fixture, which this tool does not own and must not edit to
        # fit -- carries a provider_connections-only DB that legitimately
        # predates migration 138_dario_fallback_backend.sql. A missing table
        # is a structural fact about a DB's schema VERSION, not an unknown,
        # whenever it is accompanied by independent positive evidence that
        # this really is a readable, on-topic OmniRoute DB: the
        # tier1_credential_absent query against provider_connections, just
        # above, having itself succeeded (provider_err is None) is exactly
        # that evidence. Only when that positive evidence is ALSO missing
        # (provider_connections itself unreadable, so this DB cannot even be
        # confirmed as a genuine OmniRoute DB) does an absent
        # upstream_proxy_config fall back to failing closed on genuine
        # uncertainty, exactly as every other branch here does.
        table_rows, table_err = _query_omniroute(
            db_path,
            "SELECT 1 FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table' AND name='upstream_proxy_config'",
        )
        if table_err is not None:
            sidecar_db_ok = False
            sidecar_db_reason = (
                "OmniRoute DB at '%s' could not be queried for upstream_proxy_config's "
                "existence (%s) -- cannot positively rule out a delegated sidecar" % (db_path, table_err)
            )
        elif not table_rows:
            if provider_err is None:
                sidecar_db_ok = True
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "upstream_proxy_config does not exist in this OmniRoute DB (schema "
                    "predates migration 138_dario_fallback_backend.sql) -- "
                    "provider_connections is present and queryable, confirming this is a "
                    "genuine (if older) OmniRoute DB whose schema has no delegated-sidecar "
                    "concept at all, so none can exist"
                )
            else:
                sidecar_db_ok = False
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "OmniRoute DB at '%s' has no upstream_proxy_config table AND "
                    "provider_connections is also unreadable (%s) -- cannot confirm this "
                    "is a genuine OmniRoute DB, so a delegated sidecar cannot be ruled "
                    "out" % (db_path, provider_err)
                )
        else:
            proxy_rows, proxy_err = _query_omniroute(
                db_path,
                "SELECT provider_id, mode, fallback_backend FROM upstream_proxy_config "
                "WHERE mode IN (%s)" % ", ".join("?" for _ in SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES),
                tuple(SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES),
            )
            if proxy_err is not None:
                sidecar_db_ok = False
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "OmniRoute DB at '%s' could not be queried for a delegated-sidecar "
                    "connection in upstream_proxy_config (%s) -- cannot positively rule "
                    "one out" % (db_path, proxy_err)
                )
            elif proxy_rows:
                found = ", ".join(
                    "%s (mode=%s%s)" % (
                        pid, m, (", fallback_backend=%s" % fb) if m == "fallback" else "",
                    )
                    for pid, m, fb in proxy_rows
                )
                sidecar_db_ok = False
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "OmniRoute DB has %d upstream_proxy_config row(s) delegating to an "
                    "embedded proxy (%s) -- a delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI or Dario) can "
                    "hold a Claude credential independently of OmniRoute's own token "
                    "storage" % (len(proxy_rows), found)
                )
            else:
                sidecar_db_ok = True
                sidecar_db_reason = ""

    checks.append(("tier1_credential_absent", tier1_ok, tier1_reason))

    anthropic_model = os.environ.get("ANTHROPIC_MODEL") or ""
    if not anthropic_model:
        checks.append((
            "anthropic_model_pinned", False,
            "ANTHROPIC_MODEL is not set -- Claude Code emits bare claude-* model IDs by "
            "default, which OmniRoute has an explicit branch to route to provider 'claude' "
            "if a connection exists (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-"
            "isolation.md S5)",
        ))
    elif "/" not in anthropic_model:
        checks.append((
            "anthropic_model_pinned", False,
            "ANTHROPIC_MODEL='%s' has no explicit provider/ prefix -- an unprefixed id is "
            "exactly the bare form OmniRoute's routing branch matches" % anthropic_model,
        ))
    else:
        checks.append(("anthropic_model_pinned", True, ""))

    prefer_flag = os.environ.get("OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS") or ""
    prefer_off = prefer_flag.strip().lower() in _ENV_FLAG_OFF_VALUES
    checks.append((
        "prefer_claude_code_flag_off", prefer_off,
        "" if prefer_off else
        "OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS='%s' -- this is a Tier-1 "
        "WIDENING knob (opposite polarity from what an operator would expect), default "
        "false, must stay off" % prefer_flag,
    ))

    sidecar_dir = _resolve_cliproxyapi_dir(cfg)
    if os.path.exists(sidecar_dir):
        checks.append((
            "no_delegated_sidecar", False,
            "CLIProxyAPI directory exists at '%s' -- a delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI or "
            "Dario, OmniRoute's own 'Claude-subscription proxy') can hold a Claude "
            "credential independently of OmniRoute's own DB state" % sidecar_dir,
        ))
    else:
        checks.append(("no_delegated_sidecar", sidecar_db_ok, sidecar_db_reason))

    provider = (cfg.get("target_provider") or "").strip().lower()
    deny = BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS | {p.strip().lower() for p in cfg.get("tos_flagged_providers", [])}
    if not provider:
        checks.append(("target_provider_allowed", False, "no target_provider configured"))
    elif provider in deny:
        checks.append((
            "target_provider_allowed", False,
            "target_provider '%s' is on the ToS-conflict deny list -- hmd never routes to "
            "it regardless of OmniRoute's own advisory flag" % provider,
        ))
    else:
        checks.append(("target_provider_allowed", True, ""))

    return checks


def _tier_routes_under_on(tier):
    """Whether a task of the given TIER is eligible for `on`'s capability-tier
    routing decision. `on` maps to the haiku tier only -- see header point 8,
    agents/heimdall.md's Model Routing table, and CLAUDE.md's 2026-08-11
    model-routing directive. tier=None means the caller asked the ORIGINAL,
    tier-blind question (bin/lib/issue_loop.py, preserved unchanged) and is
    always eligible, exactly like every state before `on` was redefined."""
    if tier is None:
        return True
    return TIER_ALIASES.get(tier) == LOW_LEVEL_TIER


def _verdict(state, checks, tier=None, session_verdict=None):
    """state decides ROUTE vs REFUSE vs WAIT; `on` additionally consults the
    task TIER now that it is a capability-tier routing decision, not an
    exhaustion reaction (auto keeps that job -- see header point 8 -- and
    still never consults tier). `switch` never WAITs: it is an explicit
    always-try state, so a failing preflight is a REFUSE, exactly like `on`,
    never a silent wait (header point 9). run_preflight's checks always run
    in full regardless of state or tier -- nothing here is ever a
    short-circuit (header point 1).

    `auto` is the one state that is actually exhaustion-gated (header point
    11): a passing preflight is NECESSARY but no longer SUFFICIENT for ROUTE
    under auto -- it additionally requires session_verdict == "crossed".
    Both conditions are necessary; neither is sufficient alone: a failing
    preflight WAITs regardless of session_verdict (95% crossed authorizes
    nothing on its own), and a passing preflight with session_verdict in
    {"under", "unconfigured", "unknown", None} WAITs instead of routing
    (capacity remains, or cannot be confirmed low, so there is nothing to
    react to yet). off/on/switch never read session_verdict at all -- callers
    pass None for them and this function never inspects it outside the
    `state == "auto"` branch below.
    """
    preflight_ok = all(ok for _, ok, _ in checks)
    if state == "on" and not _tier_routes_under_on(tier):
        return "REFUSE", 1
    if state == "auto":
        if not preflight_ok:
            return "WAIT", 2
        if session_verdict == "crossed":
            return "ROUTE", 0
        if session_verdict == "unknown":
            # Capacity could not be measured at all (tool missing, timed out,
            # or unparseable -- see _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict). Waiting
            # risks a delayed fallback if the measurement stays broken;
            # routing risks burning fallback-provider capacity, and a cold or
            # degraded cache (header point 11's headroom-measurement
            # citation), while Anthropic quota may still have been available.
            # This file fails closed everywhere else (see "MUST FAIL CLOSED"
            # below; tier1_credential_absent and no_delegated_sidecar make
            # the identical "cannot verify -> cannot proceed" call), so it
            # fails closed here too: WAIT, never ROUTE, on an unmeasurable
            # signal. This is its own branch, not a fallthrough into
            # "under" -- see header point 11 for why the two are kept
            # separately reasoned even though both WAIT.
            return "WAIT", 2
        # "under" (capacity confirmed NOT low) or "unconfigured" (tracking
        # never set up): neither is evidence of anything to react to.
        return "WAIT", 2
    if preflight_ok:
        return "ROUTE", 0
    return "REFUSE", 1


def cmd_status(args):
    cfg, corrupt_reason = load_config(args.repo)
    checks = run_preflight(cfg)
    would_pass = all(ok for _, ok, _ in checks)
    key_env = cfg.get("operator_key_env") or ""
    key_present = bool(key_env) and bool(os.environ.get(key_env))
    is_switch = cfg["state"] == "switch"
    switch_warning = (
        "FULL SWITCH: every task, every tier, routes to OmniRoute when preflight passes -- "
        "capability tier is never consulted and there is no no-train guarantee on this path"
        if is_switch else None
    )
    target_provider = cfg.get("target_provider") or ""
    noauth_note = NOAUTH_ROUTE_NOTE_FMT % target_provider if _target_provider_is_noauth(cfg) else None
    if args.json:
        payload = {
            "state": cfg["state"],
            "full_switch": is_switch,
            "would_preflight_pass": would_pass,
            "operator_key_env": key_env,
            "operator_key_configured": key_present,
            "endpoint": cfg.get("endpoint", ""),
            "target_provider": target_provider,
            "corrupt": corrupt_reason,
            "switch_warning": switch_warning,
            "noauth_route": noauth_note,
        }
        print(json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True))
    else:
        print("heimdall-fallback status")
        state_line = cfg["state"]
        if is_switch:
            state_line += "   *** %s ***" % switch_warning
        print("  state:                 %s" % state_line)
        print("  would preflight pass:  %s" % ("yes" if would_pass else "no"))
        print("  operator key:          %s (%s)" % (key_env or "(unset)", "configured" if key_present else "absent"))
        print("  endpoint:              %s" % (cfg.get("endpoint") or "(unset)"))
        print("  target provider:       %s" % (target_provider or "(unset)"))
        if corrupt_reason:
            print("  NOTE: on-disk config was corrupt (%s) -- reset to safe defaults (state=off)" % corrupt_reason)
        if noauth_note:
            print("  NOTE: %s" % noauth_note)
    return 0


def cmd_set(args):
    # args.value is already constrained to VALID_STATES by argparse's own
    # choices= validation (raises Rejected -> handled in main() -> exit 0
    # unless --strict, exit 2). Nothing left to validate here.
    cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
    cfg["state"] = args.value
    try:
        save_config(args.repo, cfg)
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback set: failed to write config: %s\n" % exc)
        return 3 if args.strict else 0
    print("state set to '%s'" % args.value)
    return 0


def cmd_check(args):
    try:
        cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
        checks = run_preflight(cfg)
        state = cfg["state"]
        tier = getattr(args, "tier", None)
        preflight_ok = all(ok for _, ok, _ in checks)
        tier_ok = _tier_routes_under_on(tier) if state == "on" else True
        session_verdict = _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict(args.repo) if state == "auto" else None
        verdict, rc = _verdict(state, checks, tier=tier, session_verdict=session_verdict)

        header = "heimdall-fallback check -- VERDICT: %s" % verdict
        if state == "switch":
            header += ("  *** SWITCH STATE: every task, every tier, routes to OmniRoute -- "
                       "capability tier is NOT consulted ***")
        print(header)

        for name, ok, reason in checks:
            line = "  [%s] %s" % ("OK  " if ok else "FAIL", name)
            if reason:
                line += " -- %s" % reason
            print(line)

        if state == "auto":
            print("  [INFO] session_usage -- %s" % SESSION_USAGE_NOTE_FMT[session_verdict])

        if state == "on" and tier is not None:
            resolved = TIER_ALIASES.get(tier, tier)
            line = "  [%s] tier_eligible (tier=%s)" % ("OK  " if tier_ok else "FAIL", tier)
            if not tier_ok:
                line += (
                    " -- '%s' resolves to the '%s' tier; state=on is a capability-tier "
                    "routing decision and only routes '%s'-tier work (lint, format, "
                    "rename, simple config, doc-sync -- agents/heimdall.md's Model "
                    "Routing table); default coding work (sonnet) and adjudication "
                    "(reviewer/verifier/security-auditor, always opus) never route "
                    "under 'on'" % (tier, resolved, LOW_LEVEL_TIER)
                )
            print(line)

        if verdict == "WAIT" and not preflight_ok:
            print("  (state=auto: preflight failed -> waiting rather than routing blind)")
        elif verdict == "WAIT" and preflight_ok:
            print("  (state=auto: preflight passed but session_usage verdict is not "
                  "crossed -> waiting rather than routing while capacity remains)")
        elif verdict == "REFUSE" and state == "on" and preflight_ok and not tier_ok:
            print("  (state=on, preflight passed, but tier is not low-level -> refusing; "
                  "'on' routes haiku-tier work only, not an exhaustion reaction)")
        elif verdict == "REFUSE" and state != "off":
            print("  (state=%s but a required check failed -> refusing rather than proceeding unsafely)" % state)

        note = _blocked_providers_note(args.repo)
        if note:
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback check: WARNING: %s\n" % note)
        return rc
    except Exception as exc:
        # check() must fail CLOSED. Any internal error is indistinguishable
        # from "unsafe to route" -- refuse loudly, never fall through to a
        # bare success. See header's "MUST FAIL CLOSED".
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback check: internal error, refusing: %s\n" % exc)
        return 1


def cmd_where(args):
    print(_cfg_path(args.repo))
    return 0


def build_parser():
    p = _Parser(
        prog="heimdall-fallback",
        description="Quota-exhaustion fallback policy gate (state/config/safety only -- no transport).",
    )
    p.add_argument("--repo", default=".", help="repo root containing .heimdall/ (default: .)")
    p.add_argument("--strict", action="store_true", help="tests only; callers never pass this")
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    sp_status = sub.add_parser("status", help="current state + whether preflight would pass (secret-free)")
    sp_status.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")

    sp_set = sub.add_parser("set", help="persist a new fallback state")
    sp_set.add_argument("value", choices=VALID_STATES)

    sp_check = sub.add_parser("check", help="run the preflight; exit code is the verdict (0=ROUTE 1=REFUSE 2=WAIT)")
    sp_check.add_argument(
        "--tier", default=None, choices=sorted(TIER_ALIASES),
        help="task tier/role for state=on's capability-tier routing decision "
             "(haiku|sonnet|opus, or an adjudication-role alias -- reviewer|"
             "verifier|security-auditor -- for opus). Omit for the original, "
             "tier-blind question (bin/lib/issue_loop.py's own call shape).",
    )
    sub.add_parser("where", help="print the config file path")

    return p


def main(argv):
    parser = build_parser()
    strict = "--strict" in argv
    try:
        args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    except Rejected as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback: %s\n" % exc)
        return 2 if strict else 0
    args.repo = os.path.abspath(args.repo)
    handlers = {
        "status": cmd_status,
        "set": cmd_set,
        "check": cmd_check,
        "where": cmd_where,
    }
    return handlers[args.cmd](args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    _argv = sys.argv[1:]
    _strict = "--strict" in _argv
    try:
        sys.exit(main(_argv))
    except BrokenPipeError:
        sys.exit(0)
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except Exception as _exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback: unexpected error: %s\n" % _exc)
        sys.exit(3 if _strict else 0)
